Thus of Roijs in/e i\Ir. Brown himself says, p. 11, ' played as a dance tune it is pentatonic,' and gives the substitutes for his version, which are clearly the more ancient forms. Mr. Brown gives as the marks of Scotch music (pp. ix., x.) 1. its modal, character, being constructed on the ancient seven modes; 2. its modulation or change of mode, which is constant; 3. almost absence of transition or change of key; 4. preponderance of minor forms of the scale; 5. almost absence of sharp Sevenths in the; 6. cadences on to every note of the scale, and double cadences closing on an unaccented note, which are simple (repeating the cadential tone) or compound (the unaccented tone differing from tlae preceding).— T/Yt/fSlator.]

